Sval: Settings' categories are Sval's

Sval's settings are its own categories (Sval spec §10.8), and several of
Firefox's setting groups belong to a different category in Sval than in
Firefox. A category's groups are its `groupIds` in `CONFIG_PANES`, and that
list is what the page renders and what settings search reports each group
under, so the groups move there and nowhere else. A sub-page's `parent`
follows its groups. Enhanced Tracking Protection's pages are not panes, and its
status card is not in Privacy and protection: Sval's cards there replace them,
and its advanced settings hold the prefs they reached (protections spec §3.3).
Home and startup becomes Workspaces, Privacy and security becomes Privacy and
protection, and Search becomes Search and Launcher, in the messages that name
them in the nav, the heading and breadcrumbs. Permissions and data shows
nothing: Sites, a category of Sval's, holds the permission defaults and each
site's permissions, and Extensions the add-on install warning (Sval decision
1484).

An address naming a category whose groups moved (from a bookmark, a restored
tab, or Firefox's own code) lands on the category that holds them:
`resolveLegacyCategory` is how `gotoPref` already resolves a moved category,
and its map is the one place that names destinations.

Firefox's import and export group is in Workspaces alone, so General's list
drops it. DNS over HTTPS is Privacy and protection's Secure DNS card, Sval's
own file built from Firefox's DoH settings, so the category does not list
Firefox's DoH group, its sub-page holds nothing, and an address naming either
lands on the card. Cookies and site data is a card of Sval's too, built from
Firefox's own site data settings with Sval's cookie choice, so Firefox's group
is not listed. Certificates and the proxy are in no category's list: Privacy
and protection's Advanced, a section of Sval's, draws them.

